Z||000018||Formula88||11-03-2001||03:24 AM||||Well, Wicca, and Paganism in general, has very many different "paths."  There are Dianic Wiccians, Garnderian Wiccans, Celtic Wiccans....  Unless you are a member of a large Wiccan organization, chances are you would take bits and pieces of each one to blend together in your own beliefs.  I'm a Solitary Wiccan, with some Celtic Wicca and Native American Shamanism mixed in.  The beliefs differ mainly due to the culture you grew up in.  <P>Also, about the Pentacle, a 5 pointed star within a circle.  The circle is for protection, and the 5 points of the Pentacle are for the 4 elements (Earth, Air, Fire, Water) and the fifth is for Spirit.  <P>There is no Satan or evil being in Pagan religions.  We believe there is good and ill in all things naturally - nothing is inherintly good or evil.  Example, in order to write his symphonies, Bach destroyed many blank sheets of paper.  Was this destruction evil?  In order to survive, we have to eat - we kill plants and animals for this.  Is this evil?  It's all in intent.<P>Wiccans believe in nature and reverance for all living things.  We (I, rather, not to step on any other peoples toes here) believe that divinity is in all living things.  The main theme is very simple.  What you send out will return to you threefold (karma), and "An it harm none, do what you will."  The key being, live your life as you see fit, but bring no harm to anyone else.  <P>(I guess I'm outta the broom closet, now, huh?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>If anyone is looking for simple, easy to understand info on Wicca, I'd recommend Scott Cunningham's "Wicca: Guide for the Solitary Practitioner" or Silver Ravenwood's "The Spiral Dance."  Two excellent books available in any Occult bookstore, or in the Women's Studies section of many chain stores (Barns & Noble, etc.)<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||TRiAD||11-03-2001||09:20 AM||||<I>"Outta the broom closes"</I> LOL!<P>Your description is exactly in line with my studies. <P>I appreciate all your input, and your open-mindedness. I am a Christian, but I try to study other/World religions, for 2 reasons. <BR>1) To break the improper misunderstandings most of the Christian world has of these, and replace it with the actual beliefs, as stated by members of that belief.<BR>2) (this is not my intent here)To give Christians a better understanding of those beliefs, to aid in witnessing to members of those beliefs. <P>I do not/will not try to convert people over the net. This is not my job, at least not in blatantly trying to do so with words. Here, I figure we can both learn a bit more about eachother, that's all. <P>So, in your own words, what first drew you to Wicca, and what do you feel you benefit most from it? How does your being Wiccan benefit those around you?<BR>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000020||8Ball||11-03-2001||04:41 PM||||Formula88,<BR>Very well put. I could not have stated it better myself.<P>Bravo and 2 cheers for you.||||reg||1
